sections.body:
`[27:29]` — a useful summary: the threshold takes ~30 seconds to initiate; the heart responds within 90 seconds;
the brain receives BDNF within 4 minutes; the immune system deploys for hours; blood pressure recalibrates for the
afternoon.

None identified yet. Strong cross-links: the humming and gargling material (this video
positions those as complementary — branch activation below the bottleneck, not substitutes);
sleep-position and pillow material; brain fog and glymphatic clearance in the sleep material;
glycation appears again in the aging material.

Attributed to Nachemson (original transducer measurements) with independent confirmation by Wilke three
decades later — the video explicitly notes the replication. Creep measurements attributed to Adams and Hutton;
fidgeting outcomes to Beach in *Applied Ergonomics*. Figures are given as measured values or ranges.

No contradictions. This is the deep mechanistic companion to the hour-by-hour schedule video, which
places the same interventions by clock time (screens at 20:00, bedroom 16–19 °C, warm feet, bath).
Cross-links to: the sleep-position video (glymphatic clearance, here by timing rather than posture);
the nose/breathing video (slow-wave loss via microarousals — a different route to the same deficit);
the forward-head-posture video (brain fog and glymphatic clearance); and the post-meal glucose video
(insulin sensitivity, approached here through misalignment rather than meal timing).
